oints", "Prediction markets price a Democratic win at 98 percent", "Well-funded independent Getty could split the vote but Castillo lacks visible resources"], "rationale": "The district's D+17 fundamentals, a 19-point lead in the only published poll, and markets pricing a Democratic win at 98 percent all point the same way; ballot-access controversy and a well-funded independent are not enough to overcome the seat's partisan lean.", "watch": "Consolidation of anti-Garcia voters behind the independent, or a late development in the ballot-access dispute, could narrow the margin even if the outcome rarely changes.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert rater lists this race; treated as safe for the party holding the seat.", "Newest usable poll is 80 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Chuy García (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Illinois' 4th District is a heavily Democratic Chicago-area seat, and retiring incumbent Chuy Garcia's departure did little to change its lean. Democrat Patty Garcia, the party's nominee, leads Republican Lupe Castillo by 19 points in the only published poll, though a third of voters remain undecided. Prediction markets price a Democratic win near 98 percent.\n\nThe race carries some wrinkles: Garcia faced ballot-access challenges that drew attention at a September debate, and independent Chris Getty has raised substantial money and could siphon votes. Still, two other independents were knocked off the ballot, and Castillo has shown little fundraising presence. The district's strong Democratic fundamentals make an upset unlikely, with the main question being the size of Garcia's winning margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9927, "margin": 21.92}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9929, "margin": 22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9922, "margin": 22.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9863, "margin": 20.